matomo (1:5.3.1-2) stable; urgency=high

  THE PACKAGE HAS BEEN RENAMED, AND THIS UPGRADE CHANGES NOTHING ELSE. The Matomo
  you are running, your configuration, your data and your database are all left
  exactly as they are. This entry exists to tell you one thing.

  The matomo package from debian.matomo.org is now published as matomo-vanilla.
  Debian distributes its own, different package that is also called matomo; the
  two install into the same directory and could replace one another on a version
  comparison alone, without saying so. Separate names end that.

  Nothing more will ever arrive under the name matomo, and no form of upgrade
  makes the switch: that is a decision, taken once, by you, in two steps.

  1. matomo-vanilla is published in a suite of its own, signed by a key this
     package did not install, so apt does not see it before you have set up
     both:

      sudo curl -fsSLo /usr/share/keyrings/matomo-archive-keyring.gpg \
           https://debian.matomo.org/repository.gpg
      echo "deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/matomo-archive-keyring.gpg] \
      https://debian.matomo.org/ matomo-vanilla main" \
        | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/matomo-vanilla.list
      sudo apt update

     The key is the RSA-4096 key this repository has published since 2016,
     refreshed and given a signing subkey; its fingerprint, to compare with
     "gpg --show-keys /usr/share/keyrings/matomo-archive-keyring.gpg", is
     4921 5857 403F 47D7 C124 1FDB 7803 9388 ED64 6CFD.

  2. Then ask for the package by name:

      sudo apt install matomo-vanilla

  As a precaution before that -- it is your call, the switch keeps both -- take
  a copy of your configuration and of your database:

      sudo tar czf ~/matomo-before-vanilla.tar.gz /etc/matomo
      mysqldump --single-transaction <database> > ~/matomo-before-vanilla.sql

  It replaces this package. Your configuration in /etc/matomo, your data and your
  database stay where they are; the database is migrated to the new release if
  you let the package do that, as before. If you had uncommented the archiving
  line in /etc/cron.d/matomo-archive, you will be asked whether to keep it or to
  move to the systemd timer. If you relied on /var/log/matomo/matomo-archive.log,
  install matomo-vanilla-logfile as well.

  Until the switch is made, apt prints a short reminder after each run. To
  silence it:

      sudo touch /etc/matomo/rename-notice-acknowledged

  What matomo-vanilla is -- upstream's release as it is, with Matomo's own updater
  and Marketplace usable -- and how it compares with Debian's package:

      https://debian.matomo.org/

 -- Edouard Gaulué <edouard@e-gaulue.com>  Tue, 06 Oct 2026 23:30:00 +0200
